  "Question": "A firm adopts a dual-class share structure that grants founding insiders super-voting rights while leaving cash-flow rights relatively dispersed. Holding firm size, leverage, industry conditions, and management incentives approximately constant, which statement most defensibly captures the theoretically expected relationship between this governance structure and firm performance?",
  "Options": {
    "A": "It will unambiguously improve performance because concentrated voting power eliminates shareholder collective-action problems and enables long-horizon value creation.",
    "B": "It will unambiguously reduce performance because insider entrenchment increases agency costs and weakens external monitoring by shareholders.",
    "C": "It is likely to produce heterogeneous performance effects: it may facilitate long-term strategic investment by reducing short-termist pressure, while simultaneously increasing the risk of value-eroding entrenchment, with the net effect contingent on monitoring quality, alignment mechanisms, and managerial incentives.",
    "D": "It will have no systematic effect on performance because voting rights affect control only, not cash-flow rights, and efficient markets fully arbitrage away governance consequences."
  },
  "Correct Answer": "C",
